group: Oh
formula: Cr(CO)6
construction: Fragment orbitals (extended Hückel)
headline: 
source: MO levels: RHF, def2-SVP · 
note: Model: order and approximate spacing from extended Hückel (qualitative) · calculation: eV, own scale · electrons: Cr 6 (neutral: 4 unpaired e), drawn 6; (CO)6 60, drawn 60; model and calculation: 66 drawn + 0 folded (0 levels) = 66 · labels in O<sub>h</sub>\n10 high empty levels drawn at the top (marked with an up arrow); 7 levels match the calculation only loosely (overlap < 0.80)
about: The extended Hückel diagram explains the calculation's orbitals: nothing more, nothing less.\n\n**The model.** Every column is extended Hückel (Alvarez parameters). The middle column is the molecule's own extended Hückel run, the side columns are the two fragments' (Cr and (CO)6). The basis is the same in all three, so the lines are an exact projection of each model MO on the fragment orbitals: a line joins a model MO to a fragment level that makes up at least 10 % of it.\n\n**Bonding and antibonding** are read from the sign of each model MO's overlap population between the two fragments, then paired per symmetry label and degeneracy, so each antibonding level has its bonding partner; a level without a partner is non-bonding. Colours mark the pairs.\n\n**Scale.** The model columns show the order of the levels and their approximate spacing from extended Hückel, not a scale: close levels are drawn further apart so they can be read, and no model energy is printed (it is in the hover text). Extended Hückel antibonding energies are qualitative and typically far too high for short bonds to diffuse atoms such as B, Be and Al.\n\n**The calculation** is the column on the right, on its own energy axis in eV (not on the model's scale). For each model level it shows the calculated orbital (or set of near-degenerate orbitals) with the largest overlap with it, at its calculated energy, in the colour of the model level it matches. A level marked ≈ matches only loosely (overlap below 0.80; the value is in the hover text). Labels are those of the matched calculated orbital. Clicking a level shows that orbital in 3-D.\n\nThe order of some empty levels above the LUMO differs between the model and your calculation. Virtual orbitals above the LUMO depend strongly on the basis set.\n\n**Total electrons: 66** (Cr 6, (CO)6 60).\n\nEach model level carries a short grey tag naming the fragment orbitals it is mostly made of (shares of at least 20 %), such as Cr d or CO π*. Ligand-field view hides empty ligand-centred levels such as CO π*; transitions into them are charge transfer, not d–d.\n\n**Caveats.** 10 high empty levels drawn at the top (marked with an up arrow); 7 levels match the calculation only loosely (overlap < 0.80). Extended Hückel puts empty antibonding orbitals tens of eV up, so an empty level more than 30 eV above the HOMO is drawn at the top of the diagram, marked with an up arrow, or not at all when it sits almost entirely on one fragment. A loose match means no calculated orbital resembles the model level closely; read its calculated energy with care.\n\n**Orbital labels.** Symmetry labels computed from the orbitals in Oh (cross-checked against the code's own D2H labels, which agreed on all 199 orbitals both sides could label, and every computed label subduces to match).
verdict: differs The model puts 1t2g below 8a1g, the model puts 5t1u below 8a1g, the model puts 1t2u below 8a1g; your calculation has those the other way round (5 of 91 pairs of the 14 levels judged). The empty levels differ: your LUMO is 9a1g, the model's is 2t2u. Δo is 11.4 eV in the model and 17.4 eV in your calculation.
